export const REFERENCES = {
  "browser": "# Using the user's own Chrome\n\nBy default the scripts start their own headless browser: fast, invisible, no setup. Many sites block it — the browser has no history, no logins and looks automated — and some only show data to logged-in users. In those cases the scripts can run in the user's own Chrome instead, as the user, with their logins and cookies.\n\n## When to switch\n\n- `inspect` prints `BLOCKED` (bot check, \"access denied\", captcha, 403 page), or `collect` stops with `blocked`.\n- `inspect` prints `LOGIN?` and there is no popup to close, or `collect` stops with `login-wall`, and the user wants data that needs their account.\n- The page shows less than the user sees in their own browser (region or account-specific content).\n\n**Ask the user first.** Their Chrome acts with their accounts, and the scripts drive a Chrome tab they can see. Don't switch silently, and don't use it to get around a captcha or a login on their behalf: if their Chrome also hits a check, ask them to solve it or log in themselves in that tab, then retry.\n\n## What the user sets up (once)\n\n1. Install the **Playwright Extension** in Chrome: https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/playwright-extension/mmlmfjhmonkocbjadbfplnigmagldckm\n2. Node.js 18+ (already needed for this skill). The scripts run the Playwright CLI with `npx -y @playwright/cli@latest`, so nothing else has to be installed. To use an installed copy instead, set `PLAYWRIGHT_CLI=playwright-cli`. It must be a recent version: old CLIs (e.g. 0.1.7) speak an older extension protocol and only time out.\n3. Optional: the extension shows a token. With `PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_EXTENSION_TOKEN=<token>` set, connecting needs no click in Chrome.\n\n## How it runs\n\nAdd `--browser chrome` (or set `SCRAPE_BROWSER=chrome`):\n\n```\nnode scripts/inspect.mjs <url> --browser chrome\nnode scripts/collect.mjs cfg.json --out rows.csv --browser chrome\n```\n\n- The first call connects to Chrome: Chrome opens a connect page; without a token the user approves it there (the script waits up to 3 minutes). That tab is then driven by the scripts. Later calls reuse it (session `scrape`, change with `--session` / `SCRAPE_SESSION`).\n- Tell the user not to use that tab while a run is going, and to **keep that Chrome window visible** (not minimized, not covered by another app). Chrome stops drawing hidden tabs, and then clicks, scrolling and lazy loading stall. The scripts switch the window to the scraping tab when it is in the background; if the window itself is hidden they stop with `tab-not-rendering` (inspect prints `HIDDEN`). Other tabs are untouched.\n- Same config, same output. `collect` runs the whole loop inside Chrome and only writes the CSV at the end, so there is no partial file during a run.\n- On macOS the scripts point the CLI at the standard Chrome location (`PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_EXECUTABLE_PATH`), because otherwise the CLI looks for the extension in Chrome's profile folder, which privacy protection can block (\"Playwright Extension not found\" although it is installed). Set the variable yourself for a non-standard install.\n\n## When connecting fails\n\n| message | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Playwright Extension not found` | extension missing, or the CLI couldn't read the profile folder — set `PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_EXECUTABLE_PATH` to Chrome's executable |\n| `Extension connection timeout` / `did not connect within` | old CLI version, wrong token, or the approval wasn't clicked |\n| `run-code failed` | the tab was closed or navigated by hand during the run, or the connection dropped — rerun; the session reconnects (one more connect tab in Chrome) |\n| `tab-not-rendering` / `HIDDEN` | the Chrome window is minimized or covered — ask the user to bring it to the front |\n\nReport the message to the user; don't keep retrying.\n",
  "encrypted-fonts": "# Text that looks right on screen but scrapes as garbage\n\nSome sites (novel readers, review and ranking sites, price pages) draw text with a custom web font that maps scrambled code points to the real glyphs. On screen it reads fine; in the DOM the characters are private-use code points (U+E000–U+F8FF) or the wrong characters.\n\n## Spot it\n- `verify.mjs` examples show boxes, empty-looking strings, or characters that don't match the screen.\n- The element's computed `font-family` points to an `@font-face` with a site-hosted `.woff`/`.woff2`/`.ttf` (often a random name).\n- Only digits, or only common characters, are scrambled — the rest is normal.\n\n## Decode it\n1. Download the font file the element uses (find the `@font-face` rule; one font is usually shared by the whole page).\n2. List its character map (Python `fontTools`: `TTFont(path).getBestCmap()`); keep the private-use code points that appear in your data.\n3. Render each glyph to an image (fontTools + Pillow; CFF fonts load more reliably saved as `.otf`) and read it with OCR or a vision model, in batches on a labelled grid.\n4. Build a code-point → character map and replace in the CSV.\n5. Check: no private-use characters left; no two code points decoded to the same character unless the font really has duplicates; visually similar characters (己/已/巳, 戊/戌/戍) checked by hand.\n\n## Things to know\n- The mapping is often fixed per font file but some sites rotate fonts per request or session. Load the page twice and compare font URLs before reusing a map.\n- Sites that use font obfuscation often also rate-limit; stop at verification pages.\n",
  "fields": "# Field extraction traps\n\nWrite field selectors relative to the row, then preview and run `verify.mjs`. Most field bugs show up as an empty-ish column, two identical columns, or values that belong to a neighbouring column.\n\n## Optional items of the same kind\nTiles often show optional badges built from the same component (e.g. \"new\", \"ships free\", \"low stock\"). A row that lacks one shifts the rest, so a positional selector (`li:nth-of-type(3)`) reads a different badge in different rows. Anchor on what distinguishes each badge instead:\n- an icon or marker inside it: `li:has(svg[data-icon=truck])`, `li:has(.badge-icon-stock)`;\n- an attribute on it: `li[data-kind=shipping]`.\nIf badges differ only by their text, read them all into one field (`{\"css\": \"li.badge\", \"all\": true, \"join\": \"; \"}`), or make one column per badge you care about with `all: true` plus a `regex` naming it (`\"regex\": \"(ships free)\"` gives the badge text or empty).\n\n## Several fields with the same class\nTwo figures both styled `.metric` (e.g. views and downloads): a plain `.metric` selector returns the first one for both fields. Use `index` (negative counts from the end, `-1` = last), but only if the order is fixed in every row; otherwise find a distinguishing ancestor/attribute (`.metric[title*=download i]`), or read the whole group and pick by label with `regex` (`\"regex\": \"([\\\\d,.]+[kKM万]?) downloads\"`). `verify.mjs` warns when two columns are identical.\n\n## Values that aren't visible text\nStar ratings, icons and counters often carry the value in `aria-label`, `title`, `alt` or `data-*` while the visible text is empty or an icon. Use `attr`. Check one row with `--html`.\n\n## Images\n`attr: \"src\"` returns the real image URL and skips lazy-load placeholders (data URIs, 1×1 spacers) by falling back to `data-src`, `data-original`, `srcset`. Images that only get a real URL when scrolled into view need a scroll pass (`next.type: \"scroll\"`) or come out empty — check the column's fill rate.\n\n## Discounts and optional sub-elements\nA price block may hold \"now\" and \"was\" prices only when discounted. A positional selector (`div:first-child`) then reads the old price on discounted rows and the current price on others. Select by the class or attribute that marks the current price and look at both kinds of rows in the preview.\n\n## Tag changes between pages\nIf a column's fill rate drops on later pages, the markup changed between pages — often the tag (a link on one page, plain text on another) while the class stays. Prefer class-only selectors over tag+class when the class is meaningful, and compare page 1 and page 2 with `--html`.\n\n## Text placement\nSome rows wrap text in a `span`, others put it directly in the paragraph. Select the element that always holds the text (the paragraph or cell), not the optional inner wrapper.\n\n## Live values\nCounts and prices that tick (likes, \"3 hours ago\", a stock price) and pictures that load late (avatars, lazy images) don't make a row read again a new row: the collector recognises a record by what it says, leaving those cells and empty ones out, and keeps the latest values. Two records that differ only in them stay two rows when they show side by side or on two pages. Set `dedupe` only when the verify report still shows duplicates, to the fields that name a record (author + text + time, or its link).\n\n## Links\n`attr: \"href\"` resolves to an absolute URL and looks at the nearest link around or inside the element. Links that open detail pages are worth keeping even if the user didn't ask — they make rows traceable.\n\n## Cleaning values\nThe field options in SKILL.md clean a value after it is read. Typical cases:\n- **A trailing link or badge inside the text** (\"… more>\", \"NEW\"): `exclude` its selector rather than stripping the words with `replace`, so the same word in real content survives.\n- **Several values in one element, separated by `<br>` or `/`**: `split` + `part`. With `split: \"\\n\"` the line breaks are kept while reading, so `<br>`-separated lines become parts. Check rows with fewer parts (a missing middle part shifts the rest — prefer `part: -1` for the last one).\n- **A label or unit around the value** (\"Rating: 4.5 out of 5\", \"1,234 comments\"): `regex` with one capture group.\n- **Text the page shortens** (\"…主...\" instead of \"… 主演: …\"): a pattern like `Director:(.+?)(?:Cast:|$)` runs to the end of the text when the label after it is cut off. `verify.mjs` prints a NOTE for values ending in \"…\"; look at the longest values of a regex column before delivering.\n- **Counts as numbers**: `number: true` turns \"1.2万\" into 12000 and \"3.4k\" into 3400. Text without a number (\"Reply\", \"discuss\") becomes empty — add `default` if the user wants 0. Only set it on columns that are always counts or amounts; for prices keep the currency in another column if rows mix currencies.\n- **A value split over several elements** (two italic parts of a journal title, several authors): `all: true` with a `join` string.\nAfter cleaning, preview and look at the first and last rows in `verify.mjs`: a regex that matches only some formats shows up as a lower fill rate.\n",
  "pagination": "# Getting more rows\n\nPick `next.type` from what the page offers, then prove it on page 2 with `inspect.mjs --click`.\n\n| the page has | use |\n|---|---|\n| \"Next\" link/button, arrow icon, numbered pager | `click` with a selector that means *next* |\n| \"Load more\" / \"Show more\" / \"View all\" button at the end of the list | `load-more` |\n| more items appear when you scroll (no button) | omit `next` — scrolling is the default (see Scroll) |\n| page number in the URL (`?page=2`, `/p/2`) and links you can't click reliably | `url` with `{page}` |\n| everything is already there | omit `next` (scrolling that brings nothing ends as `single-page`), or `{type:\"none\"}` to read the page as it is without scrolling |\n\n## Choosing the click target\n- Make the selector mean *next*, not *any pager link*: `a[rel=next]`, `[aria-label*=next i]`, a `.next` class, or the pager's last link when that is always the next control (`nav.pagination li:last-child a`). Previous/next arrows often share a class — on page 2 the first match may be \"previous\". Run `--click` twice if unsure and check the page number moves forward.\n- Icon-only arrows have no text; find them by class, `aria-label`, `rel` or `href`.\n- Never use a link to another site as \"next\" (download banners, partner CTAs that say \"See more\").\n- Full-page navigation and in-place updates both work; after a navigation the collector waits for the rows again.\n\n## Look-alike controls that are not pagination\nOn busy pages several controls say \"more\" or show arrows, and most of them are not pagination — for example buttons that swap a side widget, or tab bars (`aria-pressed`/`aria-selected`) that switch to a different feed instead of continuing this one.\nThe right control sits at the end of the target list and, when clicked, keeps the old rows and adds new ones (or shows page 2 of the same list). Verify with `--click` + `--probe`.\n\n## Load more\nClicks repeat until the button disappears or three clicks add nothing. Rows from one click can keep arriving for a while after the first ones appear; the collector waits for the DOM to go quiet (`quietMs`, default 1500) and harvests once more, waiting twice as long, after the button disappears. Raise `quietMs` on slow sites.\n\n## Scroll\n- Scrolling is the default, with or without `next`: each round scrolls the last row on screen to the top. The browser finds what scrolls — the window, a box inside the page, nested boxes, boxes inside shadow roots — so you don't pick a scroller. Every row passes through view, and whatever follows the list (the trigger that loads the next batch) shows when the last row does. At the last row it also scrolls everything around the rows to the very bottom.\n- Virtual lists (rows recycled while you scroll; the row count in `--probe` stays small) are noticed and read at every stop. A row read more than once is kept once, also when its counts or pictures changed in between.\n- No `next` and two rounds at the end bring nothing: the run ends as `single-page`. Pages reached by `click` or `url` are scrolled to the end of their rows before they are read.\n- `{type:\"scroll\", scroller, step}` forces one element and a fixed number of pixels per round. Use it only when the default visibly misses rows.\n- If scrolling doesn't move (stop reason `scroll-blocked`, or SCROLL says BODY SCROLL LOCKED), a popup is probably locking the page: add its close button to `dismiss`.\n\n## The wrapper changes after page 1\nSites often render page 1 on the server and later pages in the browser, or wrap page 1 in a landing layout. A class or id may embed the page number, or a wrapper may exist only on page 1. Check page 2 and write a selector that holds on both: a partial attribute match (`[class*=…]`), the container identified by what it holds (`:has(> …item…)`), or the item class alone with `:not()` to exclude look-alike lists elsewhere on the page.\n\n## Dead controls at the end\nOn the last page some sites keep the next arrow clickable; clicking does nothing or reloads the same page. The collector stops after repeated clicks with no new rows (`next-click-no-effect` / `no-new-rows`). That's a normal end if the page numbers agree.\n\n## How far to go\n`maxPages` defaults to 50. Ask or raise it when the user wants everything and the pager shows more pages. Be considerate: don't hammer small sites.\n",
  "rows": "# What counts as a row\n\nA row is one record in the user's terms. Decide that first, then find the element that wraps exactly one record.\n\n## Cards and lines inside cards\nA card often holds several lines with the same class (title line, meta line, action line). The row is the card, not the line. If LISTS shows one group whose count is an exact multiple of another group's count, the bigger group is probably lines inside the smaller group's cards — pick the cards. Check one card with `--html`.\n\n## Two similar lists on one page\nDetail pages often carry a small list the user wants (a handful of Q&A entries, a spec table) next to a bigger one they don't (a related-items strip). Pick by what the user asked for, not by size. Lists with fewer than 3 items never appear in LISTS — look at the page text, then probe your own selector. If the page simply doesn't have many rows (an item with only a few answers), say so; it is not a scraping failure.\n\n## Navigation, filters, tabs\nChannel bars, filter chips, breadcrumbs, sort buttons and footer links are repeated structures too. LISTS marks groups inside nav/aside/header/footer. If the main area is empty and only navigation remains (logged-out or \"no results\" pages), report that instead of exporting the nav.\n\n## Tables\n- Header rows (`th`), section/group rows (one cell spanning the table with `colspan`), subtotal/total rows and footnote rows are not data. Exclude them with `require` on a field only data rows have, or in the selector: `tbody > tr:not(.group)`, `tr:has(td:nth-child(4))`.\n- Striped tables alternate classes (`odd`/`even`, two hashed classes). Select by tag (`tbody > tr`), not by one of the classes.\n- Merged cells (`rowspan`): the shared value (category, date) appears only in the first row of each group; the other rows have fewer cells, so **every positional selector after the merged column shifts by one** in rows 2+ of the group. `fillDown` does not catch this: the shifted selector reads the neighbouring cell, which is not empty, so nothing gets filled and the column silently holds the wrong values. Read cells by a stable class or `data-*` attribute if there is one. If cells are only distinguishable by position, look at a group's second row with `--html` and handle the two shapes separately — e.g. two configs (`tr:has(td[rowspan])` vs the rest) or `index: -1` / `-2` counted from the end when the merged column is at the start — then spot-check rows 2–4 of a group in the preview against the page.\n- Multi-level headers, cells spanning several columns, or a table where the shapes above get messy: say so in the report and name the rows you could not verify, rather than delivering a column you know may be shifted.\n- When a table is grouped, the user usually wants one row per item, not one per group — unless they say otherwise.\n\n## Rows mixed with non-data items\n\"Show all\" / \"load more\" items rendered as list items, ad slots, \"sponsored\" cards, separators, skeleton placeholders: the row selector catches them because they share the tag or class. Use `require` (they lack the real fields) or `:not(...)`. `verify.mjs` flags rows that fill far fewer columns than the rest — look at them before delivering.\n\n## Records split over sibling elements\nSome pages spread one record over consecutive siblings: a title row followed by a details row in a table, `dt` + `dd` pairs, a heading followed by its paragraph. Select the first element of each record as the row (often it carries a distinguishing class) and read the other part with `{\"css\": \"…\", \"sibling\": 1}`. Check in the preview that the second part really belongs to the same record in every row.\n\n## Nested wrappers\nSome lists nest each next item inside the previous item's wrapper, so items sit at different depths. A child selector (`>`) then finds only the first level. Use a descendant selector on the item class (`.thread .comment`) and check the count against what the page shows.\n\n## Comments with replies\nA \"12 replies\" / \"View replies\" / \"展开12条回复\" button means the replies are collapsed, not absent; its count is not a substitute for them. When the user asks for comments, include the replies unless they want top-level comments only:\n- one row per comment of any depth — use this unless the user asks for another shape, so every reply keeps its own author, time and counts as columns: `rows` matching both comment and reply elements (often the same class, replies nested inside their comment), plus a field that tells them apart: `{\"matches\": \".replies .comment\"}` (true when the row itself matches — ancestors in the selector count), a \"replying to\" label, or the element's `class`. Check in the preview that a comment's fields are its own, not its first reply's.\n- one row per top-level comment, the replies joined into one field (`{\"css\": \".replies .text\", \"all\": true}`), only when the user asks for that shape: the replies lose their own columns.\nTo record which comment a reply answers — and to keep the same words posted under two comments as two rows instead of one — add a column that reads only on the comment's own row (its text or author: a selector that finds nothing inside a reply row), list it in `fillDown` so the replies below inherit it, and keep it in `dedupe`.\nPut the per-comment \"show replies\" button and the \"show more replies\" button that follows it in `expand`. A \"Hide\" / \"收起\" button often shares their class: leave it out (`:not(.…)`, or `button:has-text(\"…\")`), or each pass folds back what the last one opened — the preview warns when rows on the page go down after expanding. Check that the expand click doesn't navigate away. Open the record with the most replies when you look: only long ones show what comes after the first click (a pager, \"more replies\"). When each comment's replies have a pager of their own (\"上一页 1 2 下一页\", \"Next\"), put its next button in `expand` too: the run reads the rows after every click, so the replies a page turn replaced are kept — one row per reply works with such a pager. When next, previous and the page numbers are the same kind of button, select next by its text (`button:has-text(\"下一页\")`).\n\n## Empty and gated pages\nNo rows at all on the first page can mean: the list renders late (try `--wait 5000`, raise `waitTimeoutMs`), the page is empty (\"no reviews yet\", \"content deleted\", soft 404), it needs login, or the visitor was blocked (\"too many requests\", 403). Quote what the page says in your report.\n",
  "stop-signals": "# Judging how a run ended\n\nThe collector stops on rules; you decide what the stop means. Use the summary JSON: `stopReason`, `roundLog` (rows per round), `pageTextTail` (last visible text on the page), `lastUrl`.\n\n| stopReason | usually means | check |\n|---|---|---|\n| `single-page` | no `next`, and scrolling brought no rows (or `next: none`) | was there really no pager / load-more button? |\n| `next-control-gone` | last page — the next control disappeared | page text \"page N of N\"? |\n| `next-click-no-effect`, `no-new-rows` | clicking next changed nothing new | last page with a still-clickable arrow (normal), or the wrong control |\n| `no-rows-after-page-turn` | the page after a click has none of your rows | a wall (captcha/login/rate limit — read `pageTextTail`), or the wrapper changed and your selector no longer matches (inspect page N) |\n| `load-more-gone` | button disappeared — normal end | |\n| `load-more-no-new-rows` | clicks stopped adding rows | button stuck on \"Loading…\" = hang or rate limit; or a look-alike control |\n| `scroll-end` | at the bottom, nothing new for 3 rounds | footer reached; or loading is slow (raise `stagnantRounds`/`quietMs`) |\n| `scroll-blocked` | scrolling didn't move | popup locking the page → `dismiss` |\n| `no-rows-on-first-page` | rows never appeared | late render (raise `waitTimeoutMs`/`initialWaitMs`), empty or gated page, wrong selector |\n| `max-pages` / `max-rows` | your limit, and the next control was still there | tell the user there's more |\n| `blocked` | no rows, and the page looks like a bot check / block page (`note` has the evidence: challenge element, wording, HTTP status) | offer the user's own Chrome (browser.md); if that is blocked too, stop and report |\n| `login-wall` | no rows, and the page asks to log in | the user logs in — in their own Chrome (browser.md) |\n| `tab-not-rendering` | `--browser chrome` only: the Chrome window is minimized or covered, so the page stopped drawing | ask the user to bring the window to the front and keep it visible, then rerun |\n| `setup-failed` | a required setup step could not run (`note` names it) | the button text/selector differs (language, A/B layout), the page is a wall, or the step needs `optional: true` |\n\n## `aiStop`: when a second look decided\n\nSome hosts have the end of the list looked at before a rule stop takes effect, and the summary then carries `aiStop`: `outcome` is how that look ended the run, `verdicts` each answer, `userMessage` its one sentence for the user.\n- `confirmed-complete`: the page showed the end (a \"no more\" line, the last page, a matching total). Report it as complete.\n- `possibly-incomplete`: more was expected but nothing on the page brought it. Keep the rows and say the list may go on.\n- `wall`: a login or a check stopped the list. Say so, as for the `wall` field below.\n- `rules (…)`: no answer; read `stopReason` as usual.\n`stopReason` stays the rule that wanted to stop.\n\n## Walls\nText like \"verify you are human\", \"slide to complete the puzzle\", \"安全验证\", \"访问过于频繁\", \"too many requests\", \"access denied\", \"sign in to continue\", or a login form in `pageTextTail` means the site stopped serving data. Keep the rows you have and tell the user where and why it stopped. Don't solve captchas or fake a login yourself. What you can offer: running in the user's own Chrome ([browser.md](browser.md)), where many bot checks don't trigger and the user can log in or pass a check themselves.\n\n## Totals that don't add up\nPages often claim a total (\"2,418 reviews\", \"1,234 results\") that you can't actually load: replies counted in the total, hidden or deleted items, a cap on how far you can page. Report both numbers; don't keep retrying to reach the claim.\n\n## Short pages in the middle\nIf `roundLog` shows most pages giving 20 rows and one page giving 7 that isn't the last, rows probably rendered late on that page — raise `waitTimeoutMs` or add a `waitFor` that only matches when the page is complete, and rerun.\n\n## Good final report\n\"Collected 862 reviews — all that load; the header claims 2,418 (it likely counts ratings without text). Stopped at the bottom of the list after scrolling stopped adding rows.\" / \"Collected 45 listings from pages 1–5. Page 6 asked to sign in, so the run stopped there.\"\n\n## The `wall` field\nWhen a run stalls or ends after collecting rows (no new rows, scroll blocked or at the end, click had no effect), the summary carries `wall` if the page looks like a block or login wall at that point. The stop reason stays as it was, because a popup saying \"log in to see more\" may just need closing (`dismiss`). Look at `wall.evidence` and the page text, then decide: popup to dismiss, a wall that appeared mid-run (keep the rows, tell the user), or a rate limit (wait, or slower `delayMs`).\n",
}
export const SKILL_CONFIG = "## Config\n\n```json\n{\n  \"url\": \"https://example.com/search?q=lamp\",\n  \"setup\": [{ \"click\": \"button:has-text(\\\"Sort by\\\")\" }, { \"click\": \"[role=option]:has-text(\\\"Newest\\\")\" }],\n  \"rows\": \"main ul.results > li.result\",\n  \"fields\": {\n    \"title\": { \"css\": \"h3\", \"exclude\": \".badge\" },\n    \"link\": { \"css\": \"a\", \"attr\": \"href\" },\n    \"price\": { \"css\": \".price-now\", \"number\": true },\n    \"image\": { \"css\": \"img\", \"attr\": \"src\" },\n    \"rating\": { \"css\": \".stars\", \"attr\": \"aria-label\", \"regex\": \"([\\\\d.]+) out of\" },\n    \"downloads\": { \"css\": \".metric\", \"index\": 1, \"number\": true, \"default\": \"0\" },\n    \"tags\": { \"css\": \".tag\", \"all\": true, \"join\": \"; \" },\n    \"sponsored\": { \"css\": \".ad-label\", \"exists\": true },\n    \"row_text\": \".\"\n  },\n  \"require\": [\"title\"],\n  \"next\": { \"type\": \"click\", \"selector\": \".pager a[rel=next]\" },\n  \"dismiss\": [\".cookie-banner .accept\"],\n  \"dedupe\": [\"link\"],\n  \"maxPages\": 50\n}\n```\n\n| key | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `url` | start page |\n| `rows` | document-level CSS; every match is one row. `:has()`, `:not()`, `:is()` work |\n| `fields` | name → CSS inside the row (`\".\"` = the row itself), or an object — see Field options below |\n| `setup` | steps run once after the page loads, before the first harvest — see Setup below |\n| `require` | drop rows where any of these fields is empty (group headers, \"show all\" items, ads) |\n| `fillDown` | fields copied from the previous row when empty (merged cells, group labels) |\n| `expand` | selectors (CSS, or CSS with one `:has-text(\"…\")`) clicked on every visible match before each harvest (per-item \"show replies\", \"show more replies\", \"read more\", a pager inside a record's replies). After each pass of clicks the run waits for what they bring and reads the rows again: rows a click took off the page stay in the result, so a pager inside a record that swaps one page of replies for the next loses nothing — with one row per reply as well as with the replies in an `all` field of their comment's row. Each element is clicked once, unless it still reads the same after a pass that brought new rows (a per-record \"next\" the page keeps as the same element). A row read again after a click keeps the cells the page stopped showing, and its `all` fields collect every item seen. Point the selector at the control itself (the button), not at the line or box around it; when a pass of clicks changes nothing, the run tries the single control inside each match, and the preview warns if still nothing opened. Leave out buttons that hide content (\"Hide\", \"收起\"): they often share the expand button's class. For one-off actions use `setup` |\n| `next` | omit when more rows come by scrolling or there is one page: the list is scrolled by default (ends as `single-page` when that brings nothing). `{type:\"none\"}` read the page without scrolling · `{type:\"click\", selector, waitMs?}` next page (in place or full navigation) · `{type:\"load-more\", selector}` · `{type:\"scroll\", scroller?, step?}` only to force one element / px per round when the default misses rows · `{type:\"url\", url:\"…?page={page}\", start:2}` |\n| `dismiss` | selectors clicked whenever visible (popups); `autoDismiss` (default true) also clicks close buttons of large fixed overlays — except an overlay that contains `rows` (a reviews dialog you are collecting from) |\n| `dedupe` | fields that identify a row (default: all fields). A row read again is recognised without its ticking counts, prices, pictures and empty cells, so set it only when the verify report still shows duplicates. Links are compared without tracking parameters (`utm_*`, `&pp=`, `&si=`, …) and `#fragment`; the CSV keeps them as read |\n| `waitFor` / `waitTimeoutMs` / `initialWaitMs` | wait for this selector (default `rows`, up to 15 s) / extra fixed wait after load |\n| `maxPages` / `maxRows` / `stagnantRounds` | limits; stop after N rounds without new rows (default 3) |\n| `quietMs` / `delayMs` | how long the DOM must stay unchanged before a harvest (default 1500) / pause before each page turn or batch (default 500 + random) |\n\n### Field options\n\nSelection: `css` (relative to the row; `\".\"` is the row) · `index` (n-th match, 0-based; negative counts from the end, `-1` = last) · `all: true` (every match, joined with `join`, default `\" | \"`) · `sibling: n` (read from the row's n-th next sibling element — records split over two elements) · `exists: true` (`\"true\"`/`\"false\"`: does `css` match anything inside the row — badges, \"sponsored\") · `matches: css` (`\"true\"`/`\"false\"`: does the row itself match, ancestors included — `\".replies .comment\"` tells replies from top-level comments). Cleaning below applies to `exists` / `matches` too: `\"replace\": [[\"true\", \"reply\"], [\"false\", \"comment\"]]` gives readable labels.\n\nReading: `attr` — `text` (default, visible text with whitespace collapsed), `href` (nearest link, absolute), `src` (real image URL, skips lazy placeholders), `html`, or any attribute name · `exclude` (CSS of descendants whose text is removed, e.g. a \"more\" link or a badge inside the title).\n\nCleaning, applied in this order:\n1. `split` + `part` — split on a string (`\"\\n\"` splits on line breaks, `<br>` included) and keep part n (negative from the end).\n2. `regex` (+ `flags`) — keep capture group 1 (`\"\"` if that group did not take part in the match), or the whole match if the pattern has no group; no match gives `\"\"`.\n3. `replace` — `[[\"pattern\", \"replacement\"], …]`, regex, global.\n4. `number: true` — first number in the text: drops thousands separators and keeps the digits as written (`9.0` stays `9.0`); with a `k`/`M`/`B` or `千`/`万`/`亿` suffix it computes the value; no number gives `\"\"`.\n5. `default` — value when the result is empty.\n\nClean only what the page decorates (labels, units, separators). Keep the value the user sees; don't compute new values.\n\n### Setup\n\nSteps run in order after the page loads (and after popups are dismissed), before the first harvest. Selectors here are Playwright selectors: CSS plus `:has-text(\"…\")` and `text=…`, so you can target buttons by their label. When several elements match a click (here, in `next` or in `--click`), the one on screen is clicked — not a carousel's other slides or the page under a dialog; `--probe` shows how many matches are on screen.\n\n`{\"click\": sel}` · `{\"goto\": url}` (absolute or relative) · `{\"gotoHref\": sel}` (follow a link's href — use for links that open a new tab) · `{\"fill\": sel, \"text\": \"…\", \"submit\": true}` · `{\"select\": sel, \"value\": \"…\"}` · `{\"press\": \"Enter\"}` · `{\"hover\": sel}` · `{\"scrollTo\": sel}` · `{\"waitFor\": sel}` · `{\"wait\": ms}`. Every step takes `\"optional\": true` (skip if it fails, e.g. a banner that only sometimes shows) and `\"timeoutMs\"` (default 10000).\n\nA required step that fails stops the run with `setup-failed`; the summary's `note` names the step. Setup runs once: if paging reloads the page (`next.type: \"url\"`, or a click that navigates) and the setting is lost, put it in the URL instead.\n\n## Selectors that last\n\n- Anchor on the list container and the item's own class or attributes (`role`, `data-testid`, `aria-*`). Avoid `nth-child` positions and long ancestor chains — they break when a page adds a wrapper or an optional item.\n- Hashed classes (`css-1x2y3z`) are fine if page 2 uses the same ones. Check, don't assume.\n- Exclude look-alike lists with `:not()`: `ul.cards:not(.promo) > li`, `section:not(.sidebar *) > article`.\n- Prefer a descendant (`.thread .comment`) over a child (`>`) combinator when items sit at different nesting depths.\n- Web components: selectors match inside open shadow roots, and a descendant selector (a space) crosses shadow boundaries the way Playwright's does: `load-more button` finds the button inside `load-more`'s shadow root, and a field `#replies .author` reads only the authors inside `#replies`, whatever shadow roots lie between. A child selector (`>`) does not cross. `--html` shows shadow content as `<template shadowrootmode=\"open\">`. Closed shadow roots can't be read; use the host's attributes.\n\n## Stop reasons\n\n`single-page` · `blocked` · `login-wall` · `tab-not-rendering` · `setup-failed` · `next-control-gone` · `next-click-no-effect` · `no-rows-after-page-turn` · `no-new-rows` · `load-more-gone` · `load-more-no-new-rows` · `scroll-end` · `scroll-blocked` · `url-page-empty` · `url-page-no-new-rows` · `max-pages` (the next control was still there) · `max-rows` · `no-rows-on-first-page` · `error`. `roundLog` keeps the first and last 6 rounds; `{\"skipped\": n}` marks the rounds left out in between. What each one usually means: [references/stop-signals.md](references/stop-signals.md)."
